WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.970
Trump's approach towards Russia has been one
of the most controversial parts of his short

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.200
time in office so far.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.230
It seems that the new US president wants to
develop a new friendship with this old rival....

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:21.570
The thing is that this isn’t proving so
easy, and that’s because Beijing is standing

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:22.570
between them.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.150
Russia, Putin, Moscow.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.720
Without any doubt, one of the most controversial
issues is the fascination Trump seems to have

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.009
with Russia and its charismatic leader: Vladimir
Putin.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:41.360
Insinuations, messages of support, the election
of senior officials who were close 

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:50.840
to 
the Kremlin…

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.700
The question is: Is Trump trying to seduce
the Russian leadership?

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.600
Or, as some are saying: Is there something
suspicious about this developing relationship?

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.460
It would be fair to say that Trump's approach
to Russia could have an explanation: According

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.750
to some analysts, advisers in the White House
may have told Trump to try to be remembered

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.581
and make history, by being the leader who
has the same success with Russian as Nixon

00:01:12.581 --> 00:01:15.610
and Kissinger had with China.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:20.110
NIXON SEDUCES MAO'S CHINA

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.790
On the 21st February 1972, one of the most
important events in decades took place.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.720
That day, Richard Nixon arrived in Beijing.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:39.100
It was the first ever visit to Communist China
by a U.S President.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:46.500
That entire trip was broadcast around the
world on TV, and it was major news.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.470
Millions of people held their breaths as they
saw Nixon descending the stairs of the Air

00:01:50.470 --> 00:02:03.990
Force One and setting foot in China’s capital
city.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.110
But before we continue, let´s go backwards
a bit more to see what this visit really meant.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.110
What were the goals?

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.209
And how was it made possible?

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:14.840
We’re going to travel back in time to October
the 1st 1949.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.090
That was the day that China was declared the
”People’s Republic of China”.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:24.580
The communists led by Mao had won Chinese
Civil War and they took power of the country.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.349
This new regime was now completely hostile
towards Washington DC.

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:32.709
In fact, only a year later, troops of the
Chinese army participated in the Korean War

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:40.610
to support Pyongyang's regime, therefore confronting
the US face to face.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:45.120
As a result of this bitter and violent fight,
and its thousands casualties on each side,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.469
there were no intentions of establishing any
diplomatic relations.

00:02:48.469 --> 00:03:00.879
It would even be fair to say that from that
time on, China and the USA were enemies.

00:03:00.879 --> 00:03:05.030
In the meantime, relations between Beijing
and Moscow were very different.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.849
It is true that Mao had never got on with
the Soviet leaders, (who had, by the way,

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:11.989
supported other people to be the leader of
the Chinese Communist Party).

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:12.989
&nbsp;

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:16.849
However, despite all these differences, a
Russian-Chinese alliance was almost inevitable.

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:21.450
On the one hand, the Soviet Union needed to
control, or at least influence the other great

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:22.459
communist power.

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:27.499
On the other hand, Mao´s China desperately
needed the USSR for economic, technological

00:03:27.499 --> 00:03:28.870
and military support.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:34.430
The support was so generous in fact, that
between the year 1954 and 1959, the USSR spent

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:38.010
7% of the government’s resources assisting
China.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:45.560
Some even say that it was the largest technological
transfer in history.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.989
Despite all of this, differences started to
emerge in almost every part of the relationship,

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:54.430
and the alliance got weaker and weaker until
it completely broke apart.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:55.660
Relations were getting very tense.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:00.950
First there was the assault of the USSR’s
embassy in Beijing in 1967, and then the first

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.731
armed encounter took place in 1969.

00:04:02.731 --> 00:04:14.079
The 
casualties were numerous during the fight,

00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:17.950
and a few days later, the Soviets bombarded
Chinese troops deployed in the area.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.360
The conflict didn´t go any further but, for
days, there was a feeling in the air that

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.980
this could be the start of a significant war.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:24.980
&nbsp;

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:28.250
Moscow even considered sending troops from
the Warsaw Pact countries, which Romania objected

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.440
to.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.190
It is also said that to prevent a potential
nuclear attack by Russian, Nixon contacted

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:39.410
Moscow to make it perfectly clear that the
U.S would never accept something like that

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:49.920
happening under any circumstances...

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.380
In the end relations between the two communist
nations broke down completely.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:58.540
At the time, both Nixon and his famous National
Security Adviser Henry Kissinger, decided

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.370
to use the famous saying and make it their
own:

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:08.000
“The enemy of my enemy, is my friend”
And once they’d said it, they started to

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.410
put it into action - two years later Nixon
was in Beijing.

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.020
Relations between the USA and China moved
on slowly.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:20.750
In fact, it wasn't until 1979 with Jimmy Carter
in office that full relations with the nation

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:21.750
were established.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:26.840
But the truth is that Nixon’s trip to China
made room for a period of successful relations

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.480
between the two nations.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.930
In 1972, bilateral trade between the two nations
didn't even reach a billion dollars, today

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.430
the number is close to $600 billion.

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:45.461
The relations have been relatively friendly
and the US made a solid ally against the Soviet

00:05:45.461 --> 00:05:48.330
Union during the Cold War.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:56.220
And this, according to some analysts, is what
Trump is trying to achieve with Russia - to

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.990
reenact Nixon and Kissinger's strategy for
Beijing with Moscow.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:03.600
That would explain the pro-Russian attitude
of the current President.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:09.260
HOWEVER, things very much different now than
they were in 1969.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:16.550
THE ROMANCE BETWEEN CHINA AND RUSSIA

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:20.581
Relations between China and Russia have started
to improve since the end of the 1980s with

00:06:20.581 --> 00:06:23.460
some treaties and agreements being signed
here and there.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:27.550
But it all changed when Xi Jinping came to
power in 2012.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.260
SInce then, relations have advanced quickly.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:35.020
Consider what was said in 2016 by one of the
major players in the Chinese government:

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:40.070
“The profundity and the level of coordination
between both countries has reached a level

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:42.040
we had never seen before" - Yang Jiechi

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:46.400
And Beijing seems to be certain about one
thing: An alliance between China and Russia

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.160
would be very powerful from any perspective.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:59.780
Today, both countries are cooperating like
they never have before: in 2016 Russia became

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:03.530
the first Chinese oil supplier, an oil which
arrives by land.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:08.740
This is something incredibly important for
China, since the US would be unable to block

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:09.740
its arrival in case of a conflict.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:17.470
This is something the US can do for oil that
arrives by sea.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:28.210
Also, both countries have started to conduct
joint military exercises, have created economical

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:32.150
and technological bonds, and have agreed to
carry out projects to build infrastructure

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:34.190
which will connect both countries.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:35.780
So how about some examples...

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:37.480
Well, here are three:

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:47.350
First: Military exercises
Second: Economic bonds

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:52.230
Third: Infrastructure.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:08:04.620
And for this one, what better example is there
than this…

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.710
But there is more.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.240
These two countries have signed important
military agreements.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:17.420
China has committed to spending billions of
dollars to buy some of the most modern equipment

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:23.520
the Russian army has, such as S-400 surface-to-air
missiles, and the most impressive of Russian

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.670
weaponry: the Sukhoi 35 fighter aircraft.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:32.400
Just have a look at this massive aircraft...

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:40.740
Further to this there are also many economic,
political, monetary agreements…

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:46.260
All of this has led to quite a lot of concern
in the United States.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:51.170
&nbsp;
THREATS TO THE CHINESE-RUSSIAN AFFAIR

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:56.430
But just sit tight a second before we all
start to get scared because everything isn’t

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.370
quite as idyllic as it sounds.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:02.810
Both powers may have come together, but there
are still many elements that separate them,

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:04.710
or might potentially weaken this unity.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:09.589
For one, China wants to be important and influential
in international politics and that implies

00:09:09.589 --> 00:09:11.790
becoming the leader of Central Asia.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:13.860
This is something Russia certainly doesn’t
want.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.480
In their view, the ex-soviets states in the
region have to remain under the influence

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:19.480
of Moscow.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.250
Further, there are issues concerning their
economic relations.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:26.449
The trade is unbalanced in favour of Russia
- Russia has become a supplier of natural

00:09:26.449 --> 00:09:30.670
resources to China for the last few years
while China has been supplying Russia with

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.680
Chinese manufactured goods, which are more
valuable.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.269
China´s position towards international trade
is very pragmatic: Chinese people want to

00:09:38.269 --> 00:09:42.339
make money, so they know it is good for them
to open their market to the world.

00:09:42.339 --> 00:09:45.569
This is an idea that Russia doesn’t share.

00:09:45.569 --> 00:09:50.950
And then there is the issue with some of Russia’s
partners, like India.

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:55.430
Russia is the largest weapons supplier in
India, and obviously, Beijing doesn´t like

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.430
this at all...

00:09:56.430 --> 00:10:02.380
So, even though these two countries are trying
to get closer and are supporting each other

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:06.750
against the US, we shouldn’t expect a very
solid relation between them.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:10.899
After all, China is eventually going to try
to become the leader in this relationship

00:10:10.899 --> 00:10:14.519
and Russia won’t just lay down and accept
that.

00:10:14.519 --> 00:10:19.149
IS TRUMP’S PLAN A FEASIBLE ONE?

00:10:19.149 --> 00:10:24.329
To be honest, it does not seem likely that
re-enacting Nixon’s strategy is possible.

00:10:24.329 --> 00:10:28.550
This is largely because China is going to
strongly oppose this relationship.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:32.120
But despite this, if Trump wants to earn the
trust of Moscow, he will have to make some

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:39.699
concessions which would be difficult to accept
in Washington: elimination of sanctions, giving

00:10:39.699 --> 00:10:43.670
in on Ukraine and Syria, the end of the anti-missile
shield... among others.

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:48.370
Putin can be very demanding and even a good
relationship was established between Russia

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:52.870
and the United States, it is not clear whether
this alliance would even be very effective

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.629
at stopping China.

00:10:54.629 --> 00:10:59.129
This is the reason why Obama preferred a strategy
of surrounding China by reaching agreements

00:10:59.129 --> 00:11:02.110
with the country's neighbours: India, Vietnam,
Malaysia...

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:06.620
And in this strategy the TPP was key, and
I say "was" &nbsp;because it was one of the first

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:09.290
things Trump blocked when he arrived in the
White House…

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:16.089
Only time will tell how Trump’s strategy
works out, but right now, it’s not looking

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:17.550
very promising.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:18.999
So, what do you think?

00:11:18.999 --> 00:11:22.509
Will a Chinese-Russian alliance be consolidated
against the USA?

00:11:22.509 --> 00:11:26.279
Or will Trump succeed in his Nixon-like relationship
restoration with Moscow?

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:31.269
Let us know in the comments below, and as
always do leave this video a like if you liked

00:11:31.269 --> 00:11:35.399
it, and don’t forget to subscribe for brand
new videos every day of the week!

00:11:35.399 --> 00:11:39.170
Thanks, and as always, I’ll see you next
time!

